/// Chapter 25 of the guide, written from md-docs/recipes/realtime.md by
/// scripts/docsite/guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page RealtimeGuide(path: "/docs/guide/realtime", title: "Real-time", description: "WebSockets, server-sent events, broadcast channels, WebRTC peers and workers: opening, matching, sending, options, reconnection and typed messages.", layout: DocsShell("Real-time", chapter: t("nav.group.data"), blurb: "Hold a connection open — a socket, server-sent events, a channel every tab hears, a line to another reader's page, a worker — and read the network itself.", source: "md-docs/recipes/realtime.md", sections: [Anchor(id: "before-you-start", label: "Before you start"), Anchor(id: "open-a-socket", label: "Open a socket"), Anchor(id: "listen-to-server-sent-events", label: "Listen to server-sent events"), Anchor(id: "talk-to-the-site-s-other-tabs", label: "Talk to the site's other tabs"), Anchor(id: "connect-two-readers-pages", label: "Connect two readers' pages"), Anchor(id: "read-a-connection-s-state-and-messages", label: "Read a connection's state and messages"), Anchor(id: "run-a-script-in-a-worker", label: "Run a script in a worker"), Anchor(id: "send-a-message-as-the-page-unloads", label: "Send a message as the page unloads"), Anchor(id: "show-what-the-network-is-doing", label: "Show what the network is doing"), Anchor(id: "troubleshooting", label: "Troubleshooting")], prevTo: "/docs/guide/data", prevLabel: t("ch.24"), nextTo: "/docs/guide/offline", nextLabel: t("ch.26"))) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("This chapter shows you how to keep a page in touch with the world while it is open: a chat that updates as messages arrive, prices pushed from a server, a cart that stays in step across tabs, two readers talking page to page, and heavy work moved off the page's thread. Every connection is declared like a `resource`, read with a `match`, and closed for you when the page that opened it leaves, so a route change can never leak one.\n\nIn this chapter you will:\n\n- open a WebSocket and send and receive typed messages;\n- listen to server-sent events;\n- talk to the site's other tabs;\n- connect two readers' pages directly over WebRTC;\n- run a script in a worker beside the page;\n- send a last message as the page unloads;\n- show what the network is doing.")

        Heading("Before you start", id: "before-you-start").h2

        Markdown("You should know how a `resource` is read with `match` and its arms ([Fetching data](/docs/guide/data#show-a-request-with-resource)), and how `state` and `effect` work ([State and reactivity](/docs/guide/state)). A connection is read the same way a resource is.\n\nThere are five kinds of connection. Pick the one that fits the job:")

        Table(caption: "Before you start") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Declaration").header
                    Table.Cell("Opened with").header
                    Table.Cell("Use it for").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`socket`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`ws(url, …)`") }
                    Table.Cell("Two-way messages with a server: chat, live editing, notifications")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`stream`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`sse(url, …)`") }
                    Table.Cell("One-way messages from a server: prices, progress, a log")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`channel`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`broadcast(name)`") }
                    Table.Cell("Messages between tabs of your site in one browser")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`peer`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`rtc(signal:, …)`") }
                    Table.Cell("A direct line to another reader's page, with no server in the middle")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`worker`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`spawn(url, …)`") }
                    Table.Cell("A script of your site running beside the page, off its thread")
                }
            }
        }

        Heading("Open a socket", id: "open-a-socket").h2

        Markdown("To hold a two-way connection to a server, declare a `socket` with `ws(…)` and `match` on it. This chat page shows a spinner while the socket connects, the messages once it is open, and why it stopped when it closes:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Chat(path: "/chat", title: "Chat", description: "Talk to the team.") {
    state draft = ""
    socket chat = ws("wss://example.com/chat", heartbeat: 20.seconds) {
        on message(m) { log(m) }
    }

    Heading("Chat").h1
    match chat {
        connecting { Spinner.sm }
        open       { for m in chat.messages by m.id { Text(m.text) } }
        closed(c)  { Alert("Disconnected ({c.code})").warning }
        error(e)   { Alert(e.message).danger }
    }
    Input(bind: draft, label: "Message").text
    Button("Send").primary { on click { chat.send({ text: draft })  draft = "" } }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The page opens the socket when it is drawn and closes it when the reader leaves. Inside the socket's block, `on message(m) \{ \}` handles each message that arrives and `on open \{ \}` runs each time the line opens — the first time and after every reconnection.\n\nA socket looks after itself:\n\n- **It reconnects.** When the line drops, it waits and tries again, doubling the wait each time up to a limit. A server that closes the line normally (code `1000`) is not reconnected to.\n- **It keeps itself alive.** With `heartbeat:`, it sends a ping that often and treats a missing answer as a dead line, which it closes and reopens.\n- **It holds what you send while it is down.** `chat.send(…)` while the socket is reconnecting queues the message and sends it when the line opens again.\n- **It reads JSON.** A text message that parses as JSON arrives as a value; any other text arrives as a string. A value you send is sent as JSON, unless it is already a string.\n\nA `match` on a socket takes the arms `connecting`, `open`, `closed(c)` and `error(e)`, and an `else` for the rest. `closed(c)` is handed the close, with `c.code` and `c.reason`; `error(e)` is handed the failure, with `e.message`.")

        Heading("Tune a socket", id: "tune-a-socket").h3

        Markdown("Pass options after the address to change how a socket connects and reconnects:")

        Table(caption: "Tune a socket") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Option").header
                    Table.Cell("Does").header
                    Table.Cell("Default").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`protocols:`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The subprotocols to ask for: `[\"v2\"]`") }
                    Table.Cell("none")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`heartbeat:`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Sends a ping this often, and reconnects when no pong comes back: `20.seconds`") }
                    Table.Cell("off")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`ping:`, `pong:`") }
                    Table.Cell("The heartbeat's messages")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`\"ping\"`, `\"pong\"`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`reconnect:`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`false` to stay closed after the server closes the line") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`true`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`delay:`, `max:`") }
                    Table.Cell("The first wait before reconnecting, in milliseconds, and the longest; the wait doubles each time")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`500`, `30000`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`queue:`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`false` to drop, rather than hold, what is sent while the socket is down") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`true`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`binaryType:`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`\"blob\"` or `\"arraybuffer\"`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`\"blob\"`") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`keep:`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("How many messages `.messages` holds, the newest; the oldest go first") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`500`") }
                }
            }
        }

        Heading("Type the messages", id: "type-the-messages").h3

        Markdown("To have the compiler check what you send and type what you receive, name a type for each direction inside the socket's block with `send` and `receive`:")

        CodeBlock(#"type Incoming { id: String, text: String }
type Outgoing { text: String }

page Room(path: "/room", title: "Room", description: "A typed conversation.") {
    state draft = ""
    socket chat = ws("wss://example.com/chat", heartbeat: 20.seconds) {
        send    Outgoing
        receive Incoming
    }
    Heading("Room").h1
    for m in chat.messages by m.id { Text(m.text) }
    Input(bind: draft, label: "Message")
    Button("Send") { on click { chat.send(Outgoing(text: draft))  draft = "" } }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("With `send` and `receive` declared, `chat.send(…)` is checked against `Outgoing`, and each message in `chat.messages` is an `Incoming`, so `m.text` is a `String` and `m.txet` is an error.")

        Heading("Listen to server-sent events", id: "listen-to-server-sent-events").h2

        Markdown("To receive a one-way flow of events from a server, declare a `stream` with `sse(…)`. `events:` names the event types to listen for, and `ticks.last(\"price\")` is the latest event of that name:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Prices(path: "/prices", title: "Prices", description: "Live prices.") {
    state price = 0
    stream ticks = sse("/events", events: ["price"])
    effect { if let p = ticks.last("price") { price = p } }

    Heading("Prices").h1
    match ticks {
        open { Text("Price: {price}") }
        else { Text("Connecting…").muted }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("Each event name is kept apart, so `ticks.last(\"price\")` is the last price and not the last event of any kind; `ticks.last()` is the last unnamed message. `ticks.messages` holds the messages in order — the last 500, or as many as `keep:` says. A message whose data parses as JSON arrives as a value.\n\nThe browser reconnects a stream on its own and resumes from the last event id the server sent. Pass `credentials: true` to send the site's cookies with the request.")

        Heading("Talk to the site's other tabs", id: "talk-to-the-site-s-other-tabs").h2

        Markdown("To keep tabs of the same site in step — a cart, a sign-in, a setting — declare a `channel` with `broadcast(name)`. Every tab that opens a channel of that name hears what the others post with `.post(value)`:")

        CodeBlock(#"store Cart {
    state count = 0
    action add() { count = count + 1 }
    action merge(m: Map) { count = m.items }
}

page Shop(path: "/", title: "Shop", description: "A cart every tab agrees on.") {
    channel cart = broadcast("cart") { on message(m) { Cart.merge(m) } }

    Heading("Shop").h1
    …
    Text("{Cart.count} in the cart")
    Button("Add to cart") { on click { Cart.add()  cart.post({ items: Cart.count }) } }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("Open the page in two tabs and add to the cart in one: the other tab's count follows. A tab does not hear its own posts. `cart.last` is the last message another tab posted. A channel's state is `open`, or `closed` in a browser without `BroadcastChannel`.")

        Heading("Connect two readers' pages", id: "connect-two-readers-pages").h2

        Markdown("To send messages straight from one reader's page to another's, with no server relaying them, declare a `peer` with `rtc(…)`. It opens a WebRTC data channel between the two pages.\n\nThe language supplies the channel, not a server. Before two pages can connect, they must tell each other how — an offer, an answer, and the network routes each can be reached by. This exchange is called *signalling*. Your page sends its side out through the `signal:` function, over whatever connection the app already has, and hands what the other side sent to `link.signal(m)`. One side is the `initiator`.\n\nThis page signals over a `broadcast` channel, so it connects two tabs of one browser. Open `/room?host=1` in one tab and `/room` in another:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Room(path: "/room", title: "Room", description: "Two readers, one line.") {
    state heard = ""
    channel lobby = broadcast("room") { on message(m) { link.signal(m) } }
    peer link = rtc(signal: m => lobby.post(m), initiator: query.host == "1") {
        on message(m) { heard = m.text }
    }

    Heading("Room").h1
    match link {
        connecting { Text("Waiting for the other side…").muted }
        open       { Button("Wave") { on click { link.send({ text: "hello" }) } } }
        closed(c)  { Text("They left.") }
        error(e)   { Alert(e.message).danger }
    }
    Text(heard)
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("Between two readers on different machines, the signal rides the app's own socket or `api` instead of a `broadcast` channel.")

        Table(caption: "Connect two readers' pages") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Option").header
                    Table.Cell("Does").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`signal:`") }
                    Table.Cell("Required. A function handed each message the other side must receive.")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`initiator:`") }
                    Table.Cell("Whether this side makes the offer. Exactly one side does.")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`ice:`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The servers that help two readers on different networks find each other: `[\{ urls: \"stun:…\" \}]`. None by default, which connects readers on the same network only.") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`ordered:`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`false` for a channel that may deliver messages out of order.") }
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("A peer is matched like a socket. `link.send(value)` sends a message, and holds it until the channel opens. When a page leaves, it closes its peer, and the other side reads `closed` at once rather than when the connection times out.")

        Heading("Read a connection's state and messages", id: "read-a-connection-s-state-and-messages").h2

        Markdown("Every connection is a *handle*: a value whose members follow the connection. A `match` reads its state, and text, an `if` or an `effect` reads what arrived:")

        Table(caption: "Read a connection's state and messages") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("").header
                    Table.Cell.header { Markdown("`socket`") }
                    Table.Cell.header { Markdown("`stream`") }
                    Table.Cell.header { Markdown("`channel`") }
                    Table.Cell.header { Markdown("`peer`") }
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`.state`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`connecting`, `open`, `closed`, `error` — the arms of a `match`") }
                    Table.Cell("the same")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`open` or `closed`") }
                    Table.Cell("the same as a socket")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`.messages`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("the messages, in order: the last 500, or `keep:`") }
                    Table.Cell("the same")
                    Table.Cell("—")
                    Table.Cell("the same")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`.last(kind)`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("the last message, or the last whose `type` is `kind`, among those `.messages` holds") }
                    Table.Cell("the last under an event's name")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`.last`: the last posted") }
                    Table.Cell("as a socket")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`.error`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("the failure, which the `error(e)` arm is handed") }
                    Table.Cell("the same")
                    Table.Cell("—")
                    Table.Cell("the same")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`.closure`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("the close, which `closed(c)` is handed: `.code`, `.reason`") }
                    Table.Cell("—")
                    Table.Cell("—")
                    Table.Cell("the same")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Sending")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`.send(value)` — queued while the line is down") }
                    Table.Cell("—")
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`.post(value)`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`.send(value)` — queued until it opens") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`.close()`") }
                    Table.Cell("closes it early")
                    Table.Cell("the same")
                    Table.Cell("the same")
                    Table.Cell("the same")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`.signal(m)`") }
                    Table.Cell("—")
                    Table.Cell("—")
                    Table.Cell("—")
                    Table.Cell("hands it what the other side signalled")
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("A connection left open all day would otherwise hold every message it was ever sent, so `.messages` keeps the newest 500 and lets the oldest go. `keep:` on the connection says another number — `ws(url, keep: 50)`, `sse(url, keep: 2000)` — and `keep: 0` keeps none, for a connection whose `on message` handler stores what it needs itself.\n\nA store may hold any of these too. It opens the connection when the store is first read and closes it with the store; a page reads it as `Lang.css.state`, and the store's actions read it by its name.")

        Heading("Run a script in a worker", id: "run-a-script-in-a-worker").h2

        Markdown("To keep slow work — a language service, a parser, anything that would make typing stutter — off the page's own thread, declare a `worker` with `spawn(…)`. A worker runs a script of your site beside the page and is held like a socket: owned by what opened it, and stopped when that leaves. A store holds one for the whole app:")

        CodeBlock(#"store Lang {
    state problems: [Map] = []
    state restarts = 0
    worker css = spawn("/workers/css.js", restart: 1, timeout: 5000) {
        on message(m) { if m.type == "diagnostics" { problems = m.items } }
        on restart(e) { restarts = restarts + 1 }
    }
    action complete(text: String, at: Number) {
        return await css.request("complete", { text: text, at: at }, { supersede: true })
    }
    action check(text: String) { css.send({ method: "check", params: { text: text } }) }
}

page Editor(path: "/", title: "Editor", description: "A worker beside the page.") {
    Heading("Editor").h1
    match Lang.css {
        open     { Text(if Lang.css.pending > 0 { "Checking…" } else { "Checked" }) }
        error(e) { Alert("The CSS service stopped: {e.message}").danger }
        else     { Text("Stopped") }
    }
    Text("{Lang.problems.length} problems, {Lang.restarts} restarts")
    Button("Complete") { on click { Lang.complete("a { col", 7) } }
    Button("Check") { on click { Lang.check("a { col") } }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("The worker's side is a plain script under `public/` — the one place, besides a project script, where you write JavaScript by hand:")

        CodeBlock(#"// public/workers/css.js
self.onmessage = ({ data }) => {
  const { id, method, params, cancel } = data;
  if (cancel !== undefined) return;                       // a newer request took over
  if (method === "complete") self.postMessage({ id, result: complete(params) });
  if (method === "check") self.postMessage({ type: "diagnostics", items: check(params.text) });
};"#, title: "js")

        Markdown("The page and the worker talk in three ways:\n\n- **Ask and wait: `.request(method, params)`.** It posts `\{ id, method, params \}` and returns a promise of what the worker posts back as `\{ id, result \}`. A reply of `\{ id, error \}` rejects it, with the error's `message` and its `data`. A request not answered within `timeout:` fails — 10 seconds when unset, or `\{ timeout \}` on the request. With `\{ supersede: true \}`, a newer request of the same method fails the older one, and the worker is posted `\{ cancel: id \}`.\n- **Tell: `.send(value)`.** It posts the value and expects nothing.\n- **Listen: messages with no `id`.** What the worker posts without an `id` is a message: `on message(m)` hears it, and `.last(kind)` is the last of a kind, by its `type` or its `method`.\n\n`.pending` is how many requests are out. `.state` is `open`, `error` (a `match`'s `error(e)` arm) or `closed`.\n\nA worker that crashes — an error nothing in it caught — fails every request that was out and is started again, up to `restart:` times, running `on restart(e)` each time. Past that it is `error`, and `on error(e)` runs. `.restart()` starts it again by hand, and `.close()` stops it.\n\nPass `type: .module` to start a module worker. The script is a file of the site, so it is asked for under the base path, and a name `public/` does not have is a `D01`.")

        Heading("Send a message as the page unloads", id: "send-a-message-as-the-page-unloads").h2

        Markdown("To record that something happened even when the reader is leaving — an analytics event, a \"reader left\" signal — call `beacon(url, data)`:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Checkout(path: "/checkout", title: "Checkout", description: "Pay for the order.") {
    Heading("Checkout").h1
    Button("Pay").primary { on click { beacon("/analytics", { event: "checkout", value: 42 }) } }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("`beacon` posts a small body with `navigator.sendBeacon`, which the browser delivers even as the page unloads, where a `fetch` would be cancelled.")

        Heading("Show what the network is doing", id: "show-what-the-network-is-doing").h2

        Markdown("To change what a page does on a slow line or none at all, read `network`. Like `viewport`, it is a browser value that keeps itself current:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Inbox(path: "/inbox", title: "Inbox", description: "Your messages.") {
    Heading("Inbox").h1
    if !network.online { Alert("You are offline — changes are queued.").warning }
    if network.saveData { Text("Data saver is on.").muted }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Table(caption: "Show what the network is doing") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Member").header
                    Table.Cell("What it is").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`network.online`") }
                    Table.Cell("Whether the browser has a connection")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`network.effectiveType`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("How fast it is: `4g`, `3g`, …") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`network.saveData`") }
                    Table.Cell("Whether the reader asked to save data")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`network.downlink`") }
                    Table.Cell("The estimated bandwidth, in megabits per second")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`network.queued`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("How many writes wait for the connection, when the site works offline ([Offline](/docs/guide/offline#keep-writes-made-offline))") }
                }
            }
        }

        Heading("Troubleshooting", id: "troubleshooting").h2

        Markdown("- **The socket never leaves `connecting`.** The server is not answering at that address. Check the scheme: a page served over HTTPS must use `wss://`, not `ws://`.\n- **The socket closes and does not come back.** The server closed it with code `1000`, or you set `reconnect: false`. A normal close is final; any other close reconnects.\n- **The socket reconnects every few seconds.** With `heartbeat:` set, the server must answer each `ping:` with the `pong:` text. A server that does not answer looks dead, and the socket reopens.\n- **`stream.last(\"name\")` is always null.** The event name is not in `events:`. Only the names listed there are listened to.\n- **The other tab does not hear a `channel` post.** A tab does not hear its own posts, and both tabs must be on the same origin and use the same channel name.\n- **Two peers on different networks never open.** Give `ice:` a STUN server. With none, only readers on the same network can connect.\n- **A worker request fails after 10 seconds.** The worker did not post back an answer with the request's `id`. Raise `timeout:` for slow work, or check the script answers every method it is sent.")
    }
}
